{"module":"lgbtq","voice":"male","duration":157.9,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"Black LGBTQ history","start":0.0,"end":2.14,"words":[[0.03,0.36],[0.36,1.36],[1.36,1.94]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Some of the movement's most important strategists were gay, lesbian or gender-nonconforming, and many were pushed to the side because of it.","start":2.94,"end":10.71,"words":[[2.98,3.17],[3.17,3.3],[3.3,3.44],[3.44,3.96],[3.96,4.29],[4.29,4.81],[4.81,5.6],[5.6,5.73],[5.73,5.99],[6.22,6.68],[6.68,6.75],[6.75,7.93],[8.16,8.35],[8.35,8.62],[8.62,8.75],[8.75,9.01],[9.01,9.14],[9.14,9.27],[9.27,9.54],[9.54,9.86],[9.86,9.99],[9.99,10.19]]},{"el":"intro","text":"San Francisco's CAREN Act protects sexual orientation and gender identity too.","start":11.06,"end":16.8,"words":[[11.1,11.33],[11.33,12.15],[12.15,12.52],[12.52,12.75],[12.75,13.34],[13.34,13.86],[13.86,14.68],[14.68,14.91],[14.91,15.35],[15.35,16.03],[16.03,16.25]]},{"el":"h0","text":"Bayard Rustin","start":17.15,"end":18.43,"words":[[17.2,17.68],[17.68,18.26]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Rustin taught Dr. King about Gandhian nonviolence and was the chief organizer of the 1963 March on Washington.","start":19.13,"end":27.42,"words":[[19.18,19.63],[19.63,19.85],[19.85,20.3],[20.68,20.9],[20.9,21.28],[21.28,21.88],[21.88,22.71],[22.71,22.93],[22.93,23.16],[23.16,23.31],[23.31,23.61],[23.61,24.21],[24.21,24.36],[24.36,24.51],[24.51,25.71],[25.71,26.01],[26.01,26.16],[26.16,26.83]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Because he was openly gay, and had been arrested in 1953 on a “morals” charge, other leaders kept him out of the spotlight; Senator Strom Thurmond attacked him on the Senate floor weeks before the march.","start":27.77,"end":40.82,"words":[[27.82,28.2],[28.2,28.35],[28.35,28.58],[28.58,29.11],[29.11,29.41],[29.68,29.9],[29.9,30.13],[30.13,30.36],[30.36,30.89],[30.89,31.04],[31.04,32.18],[32.18,32.33],[32.33,32.49],[32.49,32.94],[32.94,33.4],[33.66,33.89],[33.89,34.27],[34.27,34.57],[34.57,34.8],[34.8,35.03],[35.03,35.18],[35.18,35.33],[35.33,36.02],[36.28,36.74],[36.74,37.12],[37.12,37.57],[37.57,37.95],[37.95,38.18],[38.18,38.33],[38.33,38.48],[38.48,38.86],[38.86,39.09],[39.09,39.39],[39.39,39.7],[39.7,39.85],[39.85,40.23]]},{"el":"s0","text":"He received the Presidential Medal of Freedom posthumously in 2013, and California pardoned his 1953 conviction in 2020.","start":41.17,"end":50.69,"words":[[41.21,41.35],[41.35,41.78],[41.78,41.92],[41.92,42.71],[42.71,43.07],[43.07,43.22],[43.22,43.65],[43.65,44.58],[44.58,44.72],[44.72,45.59],[45.84,46.05],[46.05,46.7],[46.7,47.13],[47.13,47.35],[47.35,48.42],[48.42,49.07],[49.07,49.21],[49.21,50.15]]},{"el":"h1","text":"Harlem and literature","start":51.59,"end":53.28,"words":[[51.63,52.07],[52.07,52.33],[52.33,53.04]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Many Harlem Renaissance figures, including philosopher Alain Locke and performer Gladys Bentley, lived openly or semi-openly as gay or lesbian.","start":53.98,"end":64.45,"words":[[54.03,54.37],[54.37,54.8],[54.8,55.57],[55.57,56.08],[56.38,57.07],[57.07,57.75],[57.75,58.18],[58.18,58.43],[58.43,58.69],[58.69,59.2],[59.2,59.72],[59.72,60.31],[60.61,60.95],[60.95,61.55],[61.55,61.64],[61.64,62.66],[62.66,62.84],[62.84,63.09],[63.09,63.18],[63.18,63.86]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Whether others, such as Langston Hughes, were gay is debated by biographers.","start":64.8,"end":70.35,"words":[[64.85,65.17],[65.17,65.56],[65.83,66.14],[66.14,66.3],[66.3,66.85],[66.85,67.24],[67.51,67.67],[67.67,67.9],[67.9,68.06],[68.06,68.69],[68.69,68.92],[68.92,69.78]]},{"el":"s1","text":"James Baldwin wrote openly about gay love in Giovanni's Room (1956), and poet Audre Lorde described herself as a “Black, lesbian, mother, warrior, poet.”","start":70.7,"end":81.54,"words":[[70.75,71.17],[71.17,71.66],[71.66,71.94],[71.94,72.43],[72.43,72.78],[72.78,72.99],[72.99,73.2],[73.2,73.34],[73.34,73.9],[73.9,74.11],[74.11,75.3],[75.54,75.75],[75.75,76.1],[76.1,76.31],[76.31,76.52],[76.52,77.15],[77.15,77.57],[77.57,77.71],[77.71,77.85],[77.85,78.2],[78.45,79.01],[79.25,79.6],[79.85,80.34],[80.58,81.0]]},{"el":"h2","text":"Pauli Murray","start":82.44,"end":83.54,"words":[[82.49,82.87],[82.87,83.34]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Pauli Murray's legal writing argued that segregation violated the 14th Amendment, an argument Thurgood Marshall's team drew on for Brown, and later shaped Ruth Bader Ginsburg's sex-discrimination cases.","start":84.24,"end":97.5,"words":[[84.29,84.59],[84.59,85.04],[85.04,85.42],[85.42,85.87],[85.87,86.25],[86.25,86.48],[86.48,87.31],[87.31,88.06],[88.06,88.21],[88.21,88.67],[88.67,89.42],[89.68,89.84],[89.84,90.44],[90.44,90.82],[90.82,91.27],[91.27,91.5],[91.5,91.72],[91.72,91.87],[91.87,92.02],[92.02,92.48],[92.74,92.97],[92.97,93.34],[93.34,93.72],[93.72,93.95],[93.95,94.33],[94.33,95.0],[95.0,96.36],[96.36,96.89]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Murray co-founded the National Organization for Women and in 1977 became the first Black woman ordained an Episcopal priest.","start":97.85,"end":106.42,"words":[[97.9,98.26],[98.26,98.98],[98.98,99.12],[99.12,99.62],[99.62,100.49],[100.49,100.63],[100.63,100.99],[100.99,101.21],[101.21,101.35],[101.35,102.72],[102.72,103.15],[103.15,103.3],[103.3,103.58],[103.58,103.87],[103.87,104.23],[104.23,104.66],[104.66,104.81],[104.81,105.46],[105.46,105.89]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Murray's private papers describe a lifelong struggle with gender identity.","start":106.77,"end":111.46,"words":[[106.82,107.22],[107.22,107.68],[107.68,108.09],[108.09,108.62],[108.62,108.75],[108.75,109.22],[109.22,109.69],[109.69,109.89],[109.89,110.29],[110.29,110.96]]},{"el":"h3","text":"Stonewall","start":112.36,"end":113.45,"words":[[112.4,113.24]]},{"el":"s3","text":"The 1969 Stonewall uprising in New York is often credited to Marsha P. Johnson, a Black trans activist, and Sylvia Rivera.","start":114.15,"end":124.27,"words":[[114.2,114.35],[114.35,115.66],[115.66,116.28],[116.28,116.9],[116.9,117.05],[117.05,117.28],[117.28,117.52],[117.52,117.67],[117.67,117.98],[117.98,118.6],[118.6,118.75],[118.75,119.06],[119.06,119.29],[119.68,120.29],[120.57,120.72],[120.72,121.03],[121.03,121.41],[121.41,122.11],[122.38,122.61],[122.61,123.15],[123.15,123.77]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Johnson herself said she arrived after the fighting began; witnesses also credit Stormé DeLarverie, a Black butch lesbian.","start":124.62,"end":132.19,"words":[[124.67,125.15],[125.15,125.56],[125.56,125.77],[125.77,125.91],[125.91,126.32],[126.32,126.59],[126.59,126.73],[126.73,127.14],[127.14,127.55],[127.79,128.34],[128.34,128.68],[128.68,129.1],[129.1,129.51],[129.51,130.13],[130.37,130.5],[130.5,130.78],[130.78,131.05],[131.05,131.6]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Their activism afterward, including founding a shelter for homeless trans youth, is documented.","start":132.54,"end":139.15,"words":[[132.65,132.87],[132.87,133.54],[133.54,134.13],[134.39,134.98],[134.98,135.5],[135.5,135.64],[135.64,136.01],[136.01,136.16],[136.16,136.68],[136.68,137.05],[137.05,137.34],[137.6,137.75],[137.75,138.64]]},{"el":"h4","text":"Today","start":140.05,"end":140.88,"words":[[140.1,140.71]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Human Rights Campaign tracking of fatal violence against transgender people in the U.S. finds that a majority of the victims are Black trans women.","start":141.58,"end":150.64,"words":[[141.62,142.03],[142.03,142.38],[142.38,142.85],[142.85,143.27],[143.27,143.4],[143.4,143.81],[143.81,144.36],[144.36,144.77],[144.77,145.52],[145.52,145.87],[145.87,146.0],[146.0,146.14],[146.14,146.48],[146.82,147.23],[147.23,147.44],[147.44,147.58],[147.58,148.19],[148.19,148.33],[148.33,148.47],[148.47,148.95],[148.95,149.01],[149.01,149.29],[149.29,149.63],[149.63,150.04]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Calls to police about people's gender or sexuality are a form of the harm the CAREN Act targets.","start":150.99,"end":156.98,"words":[[151.03,151.34],[151.34,151.5],[151.5,151.9],[151.9,152.29],[152.29,152.77],[152.77,153.24],[153.24,153.32],[153.32,154.12],[154.12,154.19],[154.19,154.35],[154.35,154.59],[154.59,154.75],[154.75,154.91],[154.91,155.14],[155.14,155.3],[155.3,155.7],[155.7,155.94],[155.94,156.49]]}]}